End tag handler dispatch scanned every registered end tag handler on every
end tag, i.e. O(elements currently open) per end tag. Registered handlers
belong to open elements in stack order and the ones a given end tag
activates are always the most recently registered (an end tag pops a suffix
of the open-element stack; `end_tag_handler_idx` already relies on removals
only ever happening at the tail), so walking from the end and stopping once
no active handler remains visits only the handlers that actually run.

Without this, documents that leave many elements open in the tokenizer's
view -- a long list or table with omitted </li>/</td>/</tr>/</p> end tags,
which is valid HTML -- go quadratic as soon as anything inside them has an
end tag handler: 100k `<li><a>x</a>` took ~3 s in Bun.markdown.fromHTML and
the same applies to HTMLRewriter element handlers that use onEndTag.

